{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation: the `habeas` CLI MUST define and parse its commands and options with `clap`. A black-box test can only check this through clap's observable behavior, and the reviewed tests do that.\n\n- `tests/cli.rs#fn[clap_defines_commands_options_and_usage_errors]` runs the real binary and checks `--help`. The output starts with clap's `Usage: habeas <COMMAND>` and lists the `mdbook`, `setup`, `status`, `info`, `author` and `help` subcommands. The hidden `hook` command is absent from help, which checks clap's `hide` attribute. It also checks that `status --help` shows the `--all` and `--verbose` options, and that the `author-evidence` alias parses as `author`.\n- The same test checks clap's usage errors for an unknown option (`status --bogus`), a missing required argument (`info`), and a conflicting option (`author x --all`). Each must exit with code 2 and print clap's message plus `Usage: habeas`.\n- `tests/coverage.rs#fn[obsolete_status_coverage_flag_is_rejected_with_usage]` checks that the removed `status --coverage` flag is rejected. It must exit with code 2 and print clap's `unexpected argument` error with usage.\n\nI read `src/main.rs`, specifically `enum[Command]` and the `Cli::parse()` call in `fn[main]`. Every command, including `coverage`, `coverage-check`, `test`, `refactor` and the hidden `coverage-process`, is declared in one clap `Parser`/`Subcommand` derive and parsed by that single call. The tests therefore exercise the same parse path that all commands use. Other commands need no test of their own: the code has no per-command parsing branch, and the text names no per-command requirement.\n\nNo acknowledged gaps apply.",
  "references": "57944706d23d6efa1af9d3206f355946eefcff23d1231a08d1fe1ddcfc107fbb",
